\description{
Quantifies how much of the entire (global) functional diversity of the
reference database a group's projected intraspecific trait variation (ITV)
actually occupies, from an \code{\link[=project_fishmorph]{project_fishmorph()}} projection, along two
complementary decompositions:
}
\details{
\itemize{
\item \strong{per trait} -- univariate functional diversity is taken as the trait
\emph{range} (the one-dimensional convex hull; Villeger, Mason & Mouillot,
2008). For each trait the proportion is the range spanned by the focal
specimens divided by the range spanned by the whole reference, on the
analysis scale (the \code{log10(x + 1)} scale when \code{log_transform} was used to
build the projection). A value of \code{0.10} means the focal ITV spreads
across a tenth of the global variation in that trait.
\item \strong{per functional volume} -- multivariate functional richness in the
\code{volume_dims} leading principal components of the frozen global space,
computed by one of two \code{metric}s and expressed as the ITV volume divided
by the reference volume:
\itemize{
\item \code{metric = "hull"} (default): the \strong{convex-hull} volume (Villeger,
Mason & Mouillot, 2008), via \code{\link[geometry:convhulln]{geometry::convhulln()}}. An \emph{extent}-based
richness -- driven by the outermost specimens only, so a handful of
dispersed individuals can enclose a large fraction of the reference
hull even when the bulk of the reference is concentrated elsewhere.
\item \code{metric = "tpd"}: the \strong{Trait Probability Density FRichness} (Carmona
et al. 2016, 2019), via \code{\link[TPD:TPDs]{TPD::TPDs()}}/\code{\link[TPD:TPDc]{TPD::TPDc()}}/\code{\link[TPD:REND]{TPD::REND()}}. A
\emph{density}-based richness that estimates a kernel density from the
individuals and measures the volume of trait space it occupies above an
\code{tpd_alpha}-quantile density threshold, so sparse outliers are
down-weighted rather than allowed to stretch the volume. Every unit (the
reference, the pooled focal set, each species) is evaluated on the
\emph{same} fixed grid, keeping the volumes comparable. Typically yields a
much smaller, more conservative proportion than the convex hull for a
centrally-clustered focal set. Requires the Suggested 'TPD' package.
}
}

Both decompositions are reported pooled over all focal specimens (the total
footprint of the focal set) and separately for each species. The per-trait
(range) decomposition is unaffected by \code{metric}.
